<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>2.9. Laplace's equation in a disk</title>
  <alert>MTH 401</alert>

<definition xml:id="def-laplace-eqn-polar">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <term>Laplace's equation</term> describes a system in a steady-state, or a state of 
        equilibrium, which does not depend on time.  For <m>u(r,\theta)</m> in polar 
        coordinates for a two dimensional disk, Laplace's equation becomes
        <md>
          \nabla^2 u = \pdtwo{u}{r} + \frac1r \frac{\partial u}{\partial r} + \frac1{r^2}\pdtwo{u}{\theta}= 0.
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        For a disk of radius <m>a</m>, the boundary condition then becomes <m>u(a,\theta) = f(\theta)</m>
        for some function <m>f(\theta)</m>. The implicit boundary conditions due to the periodicity
        of <m>\theta</m> are <m>u(r,\pi) = u(r,-\pi)</m> and 
        <m>\ds {\frac{\partial u}{\partial \theta}(r, \pi) = \frac{\partial u}{\partial \theta}(r, -\pi)}</m>, 
        along with <m>|u(0,\theta)| &lt; \infty</m> representing no singularities at the origin.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>


  <exercise workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>Find the solution to Laplace's equation <m>\nabla^2 u</m> in a disk
  of radius 2 assuming that the function on the boundary is
  <md>
  f(\theta) = \begin{cases} 1 \amp  \text{if } 0\leq \theta \leq \pi \\
    0 \amp  \text{otherwise} \\
  \end{cases}
  </md></p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let's see what it looks like.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.2in">
      <statement>
        <p>You can graph your solution using Mathematica.  However, because
  the result is expressed in terms of polar coordinates, you can do
  this using the <c>ParametericPlot3D</c> command.  To plot a function
  <m>function</m> for a radius that goes from <m>{0 \le r \le a}</m> 
  and an angle that goes between <m>{b \le \theta \le c}</m>, type
      </p>
      <p>
      <c>
        ParametricPlot3D[ {r*Cos[theta], r*Sin[theta], function, {r, 0, a}, {theta, b, c}]}
  </c>
  </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Thinking of <m>u(r,\theta)</m> as a steady-state temperature
  distribution, where is the warmest point on the disk?  Where is the
  coldest point?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="3in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the value <m>u(0,\theta)</m> at the center of the disk?  
  How can you easily determine it from the boundary values <m>f(\theta)</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
